        exp: "Rule: Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 87(2) and the landmark doctrine of Drennan v. Star Paving Co., an offer that the offeror should reasonably expect to induce action or forbearance of a substantial character by the offeree before acceptance and that does induce such action is BINDING AS AN OPTION CONTRACT to the extent necessary to prevent injustice. In commercial construction bidding, an oral bid submitted by a subcontractor induces foreseeable reliance when the general contractor incorporates it into a binding prime bid. Once the prime contract is awarded, the subcontractor's bid becomes irrevocable for a reasonable time. Furthermore, a $90,000 difference ($400,000 vs. $490,000, roughly 18%) is well within the typical competitive variance in complex electrical contracting and does not put the general contractor on constructive notice of an obvious computation error. The subcontractor is bound under promissory estoppel (Option C). Option A is incorrect because detrimental reliance under § 87(2) creates an option contract that limits the ordinary power of revocation. Option B is incorrect because an 18% variance is not so extreme as to indicate an obvious calculation blunder. Option D is incorrect because construction subcontracts are service contracts governed by the common law, not UCC § 2-205, and UCC firm offers require a signed writing."
