        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: The distinction between larceny by trick and false pretenses turns on whether the victim intended to part with mere custody/possession or full legal title (ownership). Larceny by trick occurs when an actor obtains mere possession through fraud or misrepresentation. False pretenses occurs when an actor obtains TITLE to the personal property of another by means of an intentional, material false representation of past or present fact with intent to defraud. When an authorized clerk at an equipment house executes a commercial sale on credit, issues an invoice, and delivers the goods intending that legal ownership pass to the customer on credit terms, the transaction transfers title. Fraudulently inducing the clerk to pass title constitutes false pretenses (Option C). Option A is incorrect because larceny requires a trespassory taking without the owner's consent to transfer ownership. Option B is incorrect because larceny by trick applies only where the victim intends to surrender mere possession or custody. Option D is incorrect because embezzlement requires that the initial possession be lawful and non-fraudulent."
