        opts: [
            "No, because strict products liability is strictly limited to purchasers, owners, and direct intended operators of the machinery.",
            "Yes, because under the rescue doctrine, efforts to rescue a victim from imminent peril caused by a defective product are foreseeable, extending strict liability to rescuers.",
            "No, because the mechanic's failure to place auxiliary jack stands beneath the vehicle was an extraordinary superseding cause.",
            "Yes, under the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur, because commercial automotive lifts do not drop elevated vehicles in the absence of negligence."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Under modern strict products liability law (Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 1) and the rescue doctrine, strict liability for an unreasonably dangerous, defectively designed product extends to foreseeable bystanders and rescuers. In products liability, 'danger invites rescue'—if a product defect creates an imminent, life-threatening emergency, an attempt by a bystander or third party to rescue the victim is deemed a foreseeable reaction as a matter of law. Unless the rescuer's conduct is completely reckless or rash under the emergency circumstances, the rescue doctrine preserves the manufacturer's proximate liability for physical bodily injuries sustained by the rescuer during the intervention (Option B). Option A is incorrect because modern strict products liability protects foreseeable bystanders and rescuers, requiring no contractual privity. Option C is incorrect because ordinary user negligence in industrial environments is a foreseeable intervening force, not a superseding cause that cuts off liability for design defects. Option D is incorrect because res ipsa loquitur is a doctrine of negligence, not strict products liability."
